  "bibliography": {
    "abstract": "Local government bonds (LGBs) constitute a form of spatially selective development finance, combining top-down selectivity in quota allocation and bottom-up selectivity in borrowing capacity and mechanisms under uneven institutional and market conditions. Drawing on a dataset covering 2507 county-level administrative units in China in 2022, this study integrates spatial statistical analysis, XGBoost-SHAP, and geographically weighted regression to investigate the geographical logic of LGBs. Our findings reveal that bond allocation selectivity is manifested in the concentration of county-level LGBs in urban agglomerations and developed regions under top-down quota allocation. Borrowing capacity selectivity concerns uneven bottom-up local borrowing capacity and the distinction between development needs and financeability. Differences in institutional conditions, market conditions, and socioeconomic foundations are associated with this capacity through nonlinear and interactive relationships. Borrowing mechanism selectivity captures the differentiated roles of LGBs across regions with different development trajectories. Industrial development and investment expansion show stronger associations with LGBs in developed counties, while fiscal and land market conditions gain prominence in less developed areas, and industrial associations weaken in shrinking regions. These findings reveal a geographical paradox of development finance, in which LGBs may reinforce uneven development instead of alleviating fiscal stress, and speak to geographical debates on public finance and uneven development.",
