  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The study relies on cross-sectional data from a single year, which limits the ability to observe how these spatial disparities and borrowing mechanisms evolve over longer macroeconomic cycles.",
      "what": "This study analyzes the distribution and drivers of county-level local government bonds (LGBs) across 2,507 county-level administrative units in China for the year 2022. Using spatial statistical analysis, XGBoost-SHAP, and geographically weighted regression, the research maps how bond allocations concentrate in developed regions and urban agglomerations, and how local borrowing capacity relates nonlinearly to institutional, market, and socioeconomic factors.",
      "why": "It demonstrates a geographical paradox where development finance instruments designed to alleviate fiscal stress may instead reinforce regional inequality, as industrial development drives bond capacity in wealthy counties while struggling regions remain dependent on fiscal and land market conditions."
    }
  },
